Apache Iceberg version
1.11.0
Query engine
Spark
Please describe the bug 🐞
VendedAzureSasCredentialPolicy can throw a NullPointerException on the first requests that go through a new ADLSFileIO when several threads send them at the same moment.
The affected file is identical in 1.12.0 and on main.
java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot invoke "com.azure.core.http.policy.AzureSasCredentialPolicy.process(com.azure.core.http.HttpPipelineCallContext, com.azure.core.http.HttpPipelineNextPolicy)" because "this.azureSasCredentialPolicy" is null
	at org.apache.iceberg.azure.adlsv2.VendedAzureSasCredentialPolicy.lambda$process$0(VendedAzureSasCredentialPolicy.java:50)
	at reactor.core.publisher.MonoDefer.subscribe(MonoDefer.java:45)
	at reactor.core.publisher.MonoIgnoreThen$ThenIgnoreMain.subscribeNext(MonoIgnoreThen.java:241)

Cause
In maybeUpdateCredential, the volatile azureSasCredential is published before the plain field azureSasCredentialPolicy is assigned:
private volatile AzureSasCredential azureSasCredential;
private AzureSasCredentialPolicy azureSasCredentialPolicy;
...
if (azureSasCredential == null) {
  synchronized (this) {
    if (azureSasCredential == null) {
      this.azureSasCredential = new AzureSasCredential(sasToken);
      this.azureSasCredentialPolicy = new AzureSasCredentialPolicy(azureSasCredential, false);
      return Mono.empty();
    }
  }
}
azureSasCredential.update(sasToken);
return Mono.empty();
A second thread can read azureSasCredential as non-null, skip the lock, and reach azureSasCredentialPolicy.process(...) (line 50, and processSync has the same pattern) while the first thread has not yet assigned the policy. The policy field is also read without any happens-before edge to its write. #13730 added the volatile and the double-checked lock on the credential field, but not on the field that process dereferences.
ADLSFileIO.buildClient creates one policy per storage account per ADLSFileIO instance, so any burst of parallel first requests on a new file IO is exposed, for example parallel manifest reads right after a table load.
Where I saw it
Production, Azure, REST catalog with vended credentials: a Spark based compaction job over roughly 3,300 tables per hourly run failed one or two tables per run, at random, with this exception. The step failed about 0.4 s after it started, and the next run compacted the same table. Log line before the NPE, from one occurrence:
ERROR org.apache.iceberg.azure.adlsv2.ADLSInputStream - Failed to open input stream for file <redacted>/metadata/<uuid>-m0.avro, range bytes=0-

Reproducer
A JUnit 5 test in the org.apache.iceberg.azure.adlsv2 package drives the real class through a real HttpPipeline with a stub HttpClient. In each round it creates a fresh VendedAzureSasCredentialPolicy (the provider is a subclass returning Mono.just(token)) and releases N threads together on a spin barrier, each doing pipeline.send(request).block().
Against the unmodified 1.11.0 jar:

Threads
Rounds
Result

8
200,000
31,427 NPEs in 19,286 rounds

8 and 4
20,000
the test fails in 8 of 8 runs (11 to 102 failing rounds)

The NPE message and the line 50 frame are the same as in production. With a small round count the hit rate depends on JIT warm-up (one 2,000 round run at 8 threads hit nothing), so the test needs about 20,000 rounds (about 3 s).
Proposed fix
Make the field that process dereferences the volatile guard, and assign the credential first:
-  private volatile AzureSasCredential azureSasCredential;
-  private AzureSasCredentialPolicy azureSasCredentialPolicy;
+  private AzureSasCredential azureSasCredential;
+  private volatile AzureSasCredentialPolicy azureSasCredentialPolicy;
...
-              if (azureSasCredential == null) {
+              if (azureSasCredentialPolicy == null) {
                 synchronized (this) {
-                  if (azureSasCredential == null) {
+                  if (azureSasCredentialPolicy == null) {
